// Design prototype for GitHub issue #344: a slot pool with NO compile-time
// ceiling on the number of live objects at all -- not even a hidden one.
//
// kernel/lib/growable_pool.tkb already made the PAYLOAD storage lazy (one
// physical page per chunk, pulled on demand), but its own identity
// bookkeeping stayed a pair of `[usize; N]` arrays (FreelistCore(N)'s
// next_free plus its own occupant_generation), so a caller still had to
// pick N -- and MAX_CHUNKS, and SLOTS_PER_CHUNK, and keep
// `N == SLOTS_PER_CHUNK * MAX_CHUNKS` true by hand, with nothing checking
// it. That is the "guess a ceiling ahead of time" question issue #26 set
// out to remove; growable_pool only moved it one layer down. Issue #344
// asks for the layer to go away.
//
// The mechanism is the one Linux's SLUB and NetBSD's pool_cache/uma use:
// the free list is INTRUSIVE -- threaded through the free slots' own
// memory inside each chunk's page -- so there is no separate table to
// size. Concretely:
//
//   chunk (exactly one page, page-aligned, from page_alloc())
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//   | IntrusiveChunkHeader: pool_tag, next_chunk, slot_count,   |  <- 64 B
//   |                       slot_stride, page_index, live_count |
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//   | IntrusiveSlot(T) #0: generation, next_free, payload: T    |
//   | IntrusiveSlot(T) #1: generation, next_free, payload: T    |
//   | ...                                                       |
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//
// and IntrusivePool(T) itself holds six plain scalars and not one array:
// chunk list head, free-chain head, generation counter, and three
// counters. Its whole static footprint is 64 bytes regardless of how many
// objects will ever live in it, and its runtime footprint is exactly
// `ceil(live / slots_per_chunk)` pages. The only remaining ceiling is the
// real, physical one -- page.tkb running out of pages -- which is what
// issue #344 asked for.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A PARAMETER
//
// IntrusivePool(T) takes ONE generic argument: the payload type. There is
// no N, no MAX_CHUNKS, and (unlike growable_pool) no SLOTS_PER_CHUNK
// either: `sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T))` is a compile-time constant AFTER
// monomorphization, so slot stride and slots-per-chunk are both derived
// inside this file (intrusive_pool_slot_stride /
// intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk) rather than hand-computed and passed in
// by every caller. That removes growable_pool's whole class of
// caller-maintained arithmetic invariant -- there is no longer anything
// for a caller to get wrong, which is a stronger guarantee than checking
// it would have been.
//
// (growable_pool could not do this because `sizeof(T)` may not appear in
// an array-size or generic-argument position -- it does not need to here,
// because nothing in this design is sized by a generic argument.)
//
// WHAT THE HANDLE IS
//
// IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] carries the slot's ADDRESS plus its
// generation, rather than a slot index plus generation. An index would
// need an index -> address table, which is exactly the `N`-sized array
// this design exists to delete. The generation half is unchanged from the
// mechanism this codebase already standardized on (kernel/lib/
// slotmap.tkb's occupant_generation, issues #217/#296): a slot's
// generation word is stamped on allocate, zeroed on free, and a handle
// only matches while both agree.
//
// It is deliberately a DISTINCT linear type, not a reuse of
// kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's FreelistOwner (which growable_pool reuses).
// FreelistOwner's payload is a small array index; this one's is a machine
// address. Handing one to the other's pool would be a wild pointer
// dereference in one direction and a nonsense index in the other -- and
// making them separate types turns that into a compile error instead of
// something to detect at runtime, which is this project's whole premise.
//
// WHICH POOL A HANDLE BELONGS TO
//
// The owner's first static parameter is `pool_id: addr` -- the erased
// ADDRESS of the pool that minted it (GitHub issue #345, the same
// mechanism SPEC.md's MutexGuard uses to tie a guard to its lock). Every
// entry point here takes its pool as `*IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id`, so
// freeing pool A's handle through pool B is a compile error:
//
//     intrusive_pool_remove(&pool_b, a0);
//     // static value mismatch: &pool_a vs &pool_b
//
// growable_pool/slotmap cannot see that case at all -- one pool's small
// integer index is a perfectly plausible index in another -- and an
// earlier draft of this file could only count it after the fact.
// IntrusivePoolInsertResult is indexed by the same `pool_id`, which is
// what carries the brand through a FALLIBLE acquire; without indexed
// variants the identity was lost at exactly the boundary an allocator
// lives at.
//
// FORGED ADDRESSES AND STALE HANDLES
//
// What the brand does NOT prove is that the ADDRESS inside a handle is
// really one of this pool's slots: intrusive_owner_into_parts /
// intrusive_owner_new (the round trip a caller wrapping this handle in
// its own owner type needs -- kernel/net/tcp.tkb's TcpConnectionOwner is
// the motivating shape) can pair this pool's brand with another pool's
// address. That now takes explicitly naming both pools, but it is still
// expressible, so intrusive_pool_remove validates before touching any
// memory the handle names: intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid walks THIS pool's
// own chunk list looking for the page the address falls in, then checks
// the offset lands exactly on a slot boundary inside that chunk's carved
// range. It never dereferences an address it has not already proven
// belongs to this pool. Rejections are counted in
// intrusive_pool_foreign_handle_count. The cost is O(chunks) -- chunks,
// not slots, and only on the free path.
//
// A pool that never needs that round trip can have the whole check
// deleted: linearity plus the brand would then prove everything, and
// remove becomes an unconditional O(1) unlink.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-17). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - NOT SYNCHRONIZED, the same caveat kernel/lib/freelist.tkb, slotmap.tkb
//   and growable_pool.tkb all carry: no locking of its own. Safe only for
//   a single execution context, which is a fact about callers, not
//   something this file enforces.
// - NEVER SHRINKS: a chunk's page is never returned to the page provider,
//   even once every slot in it is free, so memory tracks the high-water
//   mark of live objects, not the current count. Each chunk header
//   already maintains its own live_count and page_index for exactly this
//   future step; making reclaim O(1) rather than O(all slots) wants the
//   free chain split per-chunk (SLUB's partial-list shape) instead of the
//   single global chain used here, which is why it is not a small
//   addition to this file -- see issue #346 rather than assuming it can
//   be bolted on.
// - `intrusive_pool_ref` does NOT re-validate. It is the fast path, with
//   the same contract kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_ref has: the
//   `borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, _]` parameter proves, through
//   linearity and the brand, that the caller holds a live allocation from
//   THIS pool. A caller that round-trips a handle through
//   intrusive_owner_into_parts (the only way to fabricate one) must
//   re-validate with intrusive_pool_matches first.
// - A T larger than one page minus the chunk header cannot be pooled at
//   all. That is a compile-time property of T, and it is enforced as one:
//   see intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk's static_assert, which also pins
//   the two INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE invariants this layout depends on.
//   No runtime failure path exists for any of the three.
// - Slot addresses are stable for the pool's whole lifetime (a consequence
//   of never shrinking). A future reclaim step invalidates that, and with
//   it the assumption intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid's chunk walk relies on.
//
// Prototyped against linux_user/growable_pool/fake_page_provider.tkb, the
// same small fake page_alloc() stand-in issue #26's own prototype used, so
// that porting this file to kernel/lib/ needs only the `use` line below
// swapped for "kernel/mm/page.tkb" -- exactly how growable_pool_core.tkb
// became kernel/lib/growable_pool.tkb.
use "linux_user/growable_pool/fake_page_provider.tkb";

// Rounded up from sizeof(IntrusiveChunkHeader) (48) so the slot area
// begins on a 64-byte boundary whatever alignment T turns out to need.
// Both halves of that sentence are checked, not assumed -- see
// intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk's static_asserts.
const INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE: usize = 64;

// One per chunk, living at offset 0 of that chunk's own page -- never in a
// pool-side array indexed by chunk number, which is the whole point.
// `pool_tag` identifies the owning pool; `page_index` is kept only so a
// future reclaim step can hand the page back.
struct IntrusiveChunkHeader {
    pool_tag: usize;
    next_chunk: usize;
    slot_count: usize;
    slot_stride: usize;
    page_index: usize;
    live_count: usize;
}

// `next_free` is the intrusive link: meaningful only while this slot is
// free (generation == 0), and overwritten with 0 the moment it is handed
// out. `generation` is never reused for a different occupant, which is
// what makes a stale handle detectable.
generic struct IntrusiveSlot(T: type) {
    generation: usize;
    next_free: usize;
    payload: T;
}

// Six scalars. No array, no N, no MAX_CHUNKS -- this struct's size does
// not depend on how many objects the pool will hold.
generic struct IntrusivePool(T: type) {
    pool_tag: usize;
    chunk_head: usize;
    free_head: usize;
    next_generation: usize;
    live_count: usize;
    chunk_count: usize;
    double_remove_count: usize;
    foreign_handle_count: usize;
}

// GitHub issue #345: `pool_id` is the erased ADDRESS of the pool that
// minted this handle. It costs nothing at runtime and makes "handle from
// pool A, freed through pool B" a compile error rather than something to
// notice afterwards -- see this file's header.
private linear struct IntrusiveOwner[pool_id: addr, allocation: usize] {
    private slot: usize;
    private generation: usize @ allocation;
}

// Indexed by the same `pool_id` its payload carries (GitHub issue #345) --
// without that, the brand would be lost at exactly the boundary a real
// allocator lives at, since a fallible acquire must return a variant.
must_use variant IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id: addr] {
    OutOfMemory;
    Allocated(exists allocation: usize. IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]);
}

// kernel/lib/growable_pool.tkb's own grow result has to distinguish THREE
// outcomes (issue #295): its own MAX_CHUNKS ceiling, the global page
// allocator being empty, and success. Two of those three collapse here --
// this pool has no ceiling of its own to reach, and the one remaining
// failure is the genuinely physical one.
must_use variant IntrusivePoolGrowResult {
    OutOfMemory;
    Grown;
}

// A per-chunk record of which pool owns it, for a future reclaim step and
// for debugging a dumped page -- NOT the cross-pool check, which is the
// erased `pool_id` brand and costs nothing at runtime (see this file's
// header). A plain counter rather than the pool's own address: an address
// would need an unsafe cast in what is otherwise a fully safe entry
// point.
private let mut intrusive_pool_next_tag: usize = 1;

fn intrusive_owner_new(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                       slot: usize, generation: usize @ allocation)
        -> IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] {
    let mut owner: IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] = { slot, generation };
    return owner;
}

fn intrusive_owner_slot(owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.slot;
}

fn intrusive_owner_generation(owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.generation;
}

// The same escape hatch kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_owner_into_parts
// provides, for a caller that immediately rewraps the pair in its own
// owner type (kernel/net/tcp.tkb's TcpConnectionOwner is the motivating
// shape). Reconstruct with intrusive_owner_new. This is the ONLY way to
// produce a handle this pool did not mint, which is why remove validates.
fn intrusive_owner_into_parts(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                              owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation])
        -> (usize, usize @ allocation) {
    return (owner.slot, owner.generation);
}

// Discharges the linear obligation without returning the slot -- for a
// caller that has already established the slot must not be pushed back
// (see intrusive_pool_remove's own rejection paths).
fn intrusive_owner_drop(owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) {}

fn intrusive_pool_init(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) {
    pool.pool_tag = intrusive_pool_next_tag;
    intrusive_pool_next_tag = intrusive_pool_next_tag + 1;
    pool.chunk_head = 0;
    pool.free_head = 0;
    pool.next_generation = 1;
    pool.live_count = 0;
    pool.chunk_count = 0;
    pool.double_remove_count = 0;
    pool.foreign_handle_count = 0;
}

// Both derived from sizeof after monomorphization, never supplied by a
// caller -- see this file's header.
fn intrusive_pool_slot_stride(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
}

fn intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    // GitHub issue #344: "does a T-sized slot fit in a chunk at all?" is a
    // property of T and of this file's own layout constants, both fully
    // known once monomorphization has picked T -- so it is settled here,
    // once per instantiation, and a pool of an oversized T does not
    // compile. An earlier draft of this file carried a SlotTooLarge case
    // through IntrusivePoolGrowResult and IntrusivePoolInsertResult
    // instead, making every CALLER match on a runtime outcome that could
    // never actually occur for a T that compiled once.
    static_assert(sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T)) <= PAGE_SIZE - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE,
                  "IntrusivePool(T): one T-sized slot does not fit in a single chunk");
    // The chunk header is at offset 0 of the page and the slot area
    // follows it, so this reservation has to cover the header and land on
    // an alignment every payload type can live at.
    static_assert(sizeof(IntrusiveChunkHeader) <= INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE,
                  "INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE is smaller than the header it reserves space for");
    static_assert(INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE % 16 == 0,
                  "INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE must keep the slot area maximally aligned");
    return (PAGE_SIZE - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE) / sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
}

// The chunk a slot address belongs to, given that every chunk is exactly
// one page-aligned page (true by construction: one page_alloc() call backs
// exactly one chunk). PAGE_SIZE is a power of two, so this is a mask, but
// spelled as arithmetic to keep it readable.
fn intrusive_chunk_base_of(slot_address: usize) -> usize {
    return slot_address - (slot_address % PAGE_SIZE);
}

// The one provenance boundary on the chunk side: `base` is only ever a
// value this pool itself recorded from page_physical_address(), or one
// already proven to be on this pool's chunk list.
fn intrusive_chunk_header_at(base: usize) -> *IntrusiveChunkHeader !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { base as *IntrusiveChunkHeader };
}

// Same for the slot side. Callers hold either an address they just
// computed from a chunk they own, or one validated by
// intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid.
fn intrusive_slot_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id, slot_address: usize)
        -> *IntrusiveSlot(T) !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { slot_address as *IntrusiveSlot(T) };
}

// Is `base` a chunk this pool actually owns? Walks this pool's own chunk
// list rather than reading a header at `base` and trusting what it finds
// there -- the difference matters, because the whole point of validating
// is that `base` may be garbage, and a header read would already be the
// wild dereference we are trying to prevent. O(chunks).
fn intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id, base: usize)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let mut cursor: usize = pool.chunk_head;
    while (cursor != 0) {
        if (cursor == base) { return true; }
        cursor = intrusive_chunk_header_at(cursor).next_chunk;
    }
    return false;
}

// Full validation of a raw slot address: it names a chunk on this pool's
// list, and it lands exactly on a slot boundary within that chunk's carved
// slot range. After this returns true, dereferencing the slot is sound.
fn intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                slot_address: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (slot_address == 0) { return false; }
    let base: usize = intrusive_chunk_base_of(slot_address);
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return false; }
    let offset: usize = slot_address - base;
    if (offset < INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE) { return false; }
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(base);
    let slot_offset: usize = offset - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE;
    if (header.slot_stride == 0) { return false; }
    if (slot_offset % header.slot_stride != 0) { return false; }
    return slot_offset / header.slot_stride < header.slot_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_zero_page(base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    let bytes: *u8 = unsafe { base as *u8 };
    let page_size: isize = PAGE_SIZE as isize;
    for i: isize in 0..<page_size {
        bytes[i] = 0;
    }
}

// Pulls one more page, carves it into slots, and pushes all of them onto
// the free chain at once -- the pool's only growth step. Slots are linked
// in descending order so that the chain hands them out in ascending order,
// which keeps a chunk's first-fill behavior easy to reason about (contrast
// growable_pool_activate_chunk's own ascending push / descending pop).
fn intrusive_pool_grow_one_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> IntrusivePoolGrowResult !{unsafe} {
    let stride: usize = sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    let slot_count: usize = intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool);
    match page_alloc() {
        PageAllocResult::OutOfMemory => {
            return IntrusivePoolGrowResult::OutOfMemory;
        }
        PageAllocResult::Allocated(page_owner) => {
            let base: usize = page_physical_address(page_owner);
            let page_index: usize = page_owner_transfer_index(page_owner) as usize;
            intrusive_pool_zero_page(base);
            let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(base);
            header.pool_tag = pool.pool_tag;
            header.next_chunk = pool.chunk_head;
            header.slot_count = slot_count;
            header.slot_stride = stride;
            header.page_index = page_index;
            header.live_count = 0;
            pool.chunk_head = base;
            pool.chunk_count = pool.chunk_count + 1;
            let mut remaining: usize = slot_count;
            while (remaining > 0) {
                remaining = remaining - 1;
                let slot_address: usize =
                    base + INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE + remaining * stride;
                let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
                slot.generation = 0;
                slot.next_free = pool.free_head;
                pool.free_head = slot_address;
            }
            return IntrusivePoolGrowResult::Grown;
        }
    }
}

fn intrusive_pool_insert(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id] !{unsafe} {
    if (pool.free_head == 0) {
        match intrusive_pool_grow_one_chunk(pool) {
            IntrusivePoolGrowResult::OutOfMemory => {
                return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
            }
            IntrusivePoolGrowResult::Grown => {}
        }
    }
    let slot_address: usize = pool.free_head;
    if (slot_address == 0) {
        // Unreachable: a chunk that grew successfully contributed
        // slot_count >= 1 free slots. No trap site either way.
        return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
    }
    let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
    pool.free_head = slot.next_free;
    let generation: usize = pool.next_generation;
    pool.next_generation = generation + 1;
    slot.generation = generation;
    slot.next_free = 0;
    pool.live_count = pool.live_count + 1;
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader =
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(intrusive_chunk_base_of(slot_address));
    header.live_count = header.live_count + 1;
    return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(
        intrusive_owner_new(pool, slot_address, generation));
}

// Rejects, rather than acts on, two distinguishable classes of bad handle:
// one this pool never minted (foreign_handle_count) and one whose slot is
// already free or has been reused since (double_remove_count) -- the same
// distinction kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's slotmap_remove draws, plus the
// cross-pool case slotmap has no way to see.
fn intrusive_pool_remove(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                         owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) !{unsafe} {
    let slot_address: usize = intrusive_owner_slot(owner);
    let generation: usize = intrusive_owner_generation(owner);
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) {
        pool.foreign_handle_count = pool.foreign_handle_count + 1;
        intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
        return;
    }
    let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
    if (slot.generation == 0 || slot.generation != generation) {
        pool.double_remove_count = pool.double_remove_count + 1;
        intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
        return;
    }
    slot.generation = 0;
    slot.next_free = pool.free_head;
    pool.free_head = slot_address;
    pool.live_count = pool.live_count - 1;
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader =
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(intrusive_chunk_base_of(slot_address));
    header.live_count = header.live_count - 1;
    intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
}

// The capability kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's slotmap_matches and
// growable_pool_matches both provide, over a raw (address, generation)
// pair rather than an (index, generation) one: does this slot still hold
// this exact occupant? No ownership proof required, and safe to call on an
// address of unknown provenance.
fn intrusive_pool_matches(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                          slot_address: usize, generation: usize)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (generation == 0) { return false; }
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return false; }
    return intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address).generation == generation;
}

// Fast path, no re-validation: see this file's "Current limitations".
// Field access through the result needs no explicit deref
// (`intrusive_pool_ref(&pool, owner).field = 1;`); a whole-T read or write
// does -- the same shape kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_ref has.
fn intrusive_pool_ref(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                      owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return intrusive_pool_payload_at(pool, intrusive_owner_slot(owner));
}

fn intrusive_pool_payload_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                             slot_address: usize) -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe {
        (slot_address + offsetof(IntrusiveSlot(T), payload)) as *T
    };
}

// -- Iteration ---------------------------------------------------------
// A consumer like kernel/net/tcp.tkb does not only allocate and free: it
// scans its pool looking for the connection matching a received segment.
// With a fixed `[T; N]` that is `for i in 0..<N`; here it is a cursor walk
// over the chunk list. Both intrusive_pool_first_slot and
// intrusive_pool_next_slot return 0 for "no more", and visit FREE slots
// too -- callers filter on intrusive_pool_slot_generation() != 0, the
// direct analogue of today's `if (slotmap_matches(...))` guard inside such
// a scan.

fn intrusive_pool_first_slot(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (pool.chunk_head == 0) { return 0; }
    return pool.chunk_head + INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE;
}

fn intrusive_pool_next_slot(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                            slot_address: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return 0; }
    let base: usize = intrusive_chunk_base_of(slot_address);
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(base);
    let index: usize =
        (slot_address - base - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE) / header.slot_stride;
    if (index + 1 < header.slot_count) {
        return slot_address + header.slot_stride;
    }
    if (header.next_chunk == 0) { return 0; }
    return header.next_chunk + INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_SIZE;
}

// 0 means "this slot is free". Safe on an address of unknown provenance.
fn intrusive_pool_slot_generation(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                  slot_address: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return 0; }
    return intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address).generation;
}

// -- Observers ---------------------------------------------------------

fn intrusive_pool_live_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.live_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_chunk_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.chunk_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_double_remove_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.double_remove_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_foreign_handle_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.foreign_handle_count;
}
